<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-student-guide-short-answer-analysis"
         xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing Student Mathematical Reasoning</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Students responses to short-answer questions may be only one or two 
      sentences in length, but they can still show what information a student 
      noticed, how the student connected that information to a mathematical 
      idea, and what conclusion the student reached.
    </p>

    <p>
      This project studies those short explanations. Our goal is to examine the
      mathematical justification contained in the response. In particular, we
      ask:
      <ul>
        <li>What feature or fact did the student notice?</li>
        <li>What conclusion did the student draw?</li>
        <li>How did the student connect the feature to the conclusion?</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      This guide outlines the basic ideas of our approach.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Explain the purpose of the project.
      </li>
      <li>
        Identify the criterion, inferential link, and judgment in a short
        mathematical explanation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Describe how a response moves through the analysis pipeline.
      </li>
      <li>
        Recognize decisions that require interpretation by a human researcher.
      </li>
      <li>
        Annotate introductory examples using the Episode Model.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 1: BALACHEFF FRAMEWORK                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-balacheff">
    <title>The Balacheff Framework</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This project is motivated by the cK&#162; framework developed by
        Balacheff and his collaborators (Balacheff &amp; Gaudin, 2009). The
        framework provides a language for describing mathematical knowing as it
        appears when a person works on a particular class of problems.
      </p>

      <p>
        A central principle is that researchers should be cautious when inferring
        what a student knows from what the student writes or does. A written
        response provides evidence, not direct access to the student's thinking.
        The framework therefore focuses on the problem being solved, the
        mathematical actions and representations involved, and the means by which
        the student appears to make decisions or judge results.
      </p>

      <p>
        In this framework, a conception is situated. It describes a form of
        mathematical knowing that functions within a particular range of problems
        and conditions, not a fixed belief or permanent characteristic located
        entirely within the student.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-conception-model">
      <title>The Model of a Conception</title>

      <p>
        Balacheff and Gaudin model a conception by the quadruplet
        <md>
          (P,R,L,\Sigma),
        </md>
        whose components describe distinct aspects of mathematical activity.

        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Problems</title>
            <p>
              <m>P</m> is the class of problems or situations that the conception
              can successfully address.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Operators</title>
            <p>
              <m>R</m> is the set of mathematical actions used to work on a
              problem or transform a representation in <m>L</m>. Examples include
              row reducing a matrix, substituting into an equation,
              differentiating a function, and rearranging a symbolic expression.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Representation System</title>
            <p>
              <m>L</m> is the linguistic, symbolic, graphical, or other system
              through which mathematical actions, feedback, and results are
              expressed. Examples include equations, matrices, diagrams, written
              mathematical language, and software interfaces.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Control Structure</title>
            <p>
              <m>\Sigma</m> is the means used to make choices, evaluate actions,
              validate results, and decide whether a problem has been solved.
              Examples include applying a definition, checking a computation,
              recognizing that a system is consistent, and using the presence of
              a free variable to classify the number of solutions.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Here, <m>R</m> denotes <em>operators</em>, not representations;
        Balacheff and Gaudin use <m>L</m> for the representation system.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-control-structures">
      <title>Control Structures</title>

      <p>
        This project focuses on evidence for control structures in students'
        written justifications. Short responses rarely show every mathematical
        operation a student performed, but they often identify a feature the
        student noticed and the conclusion drawn from it.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, a student might justify a conclusion using the presence of a
        free variable, the number of pivots, the consistency of a system, or the
        sign of a derivative. Such features function as
        <term>criteria</term> in the student's reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        A criterion and a control structure are related but distinct. A criterion
        is a particular condition or feature identified in a response. A control
        structure is the broader means by which such information is used to make
        a decision or validate a conclusion. The <term>Episode Model</term>,
        introduced later in this guide, records how a criterion is connected to a
        judgment in a particular response.
      </p>
    </subsection>
    
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-operation-control">
      <title>Operations and Controls</title>

      <p>
        An <term>operation</term> creates or transforms a mathematical object. A
        <term>control</term> interprets a result and supports a decision.
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Operation</title>
            <p><q>I row reduced the augmented matrix.</q></p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Control</title>
            <p>
              <q>The reduced matrix has no contradictory row, so the system is
              consistent.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The first statement reports an action. The second evaluates the result and
        draws a judgment. Operations may still provide the criterion used in an episode.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-balacheff-limits">
      <title>What We Can and Cannot Claim</title>

      <p>
        A short response may provide evidence that a student used a particular
        mathematical criterion or enacted a particular control structure. It
        rarely provides enough evidence to reconstruct an entire conception,
        including its full range of problems, operators, representations, and
        controls.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project therefore uses language such as:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <q>the response provides evidence for a control structure,</q>
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>the student appears to use the following criterion,</q> or
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>the written justification contains the following episode.</q>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        It avoids statements such as:
        <ul>
          <li><q>the tool detected the student's conception,</q></li>
          <li><q>the student believes this in every situation,</q> or</li>
          <li>
            <q>the response gives a complete model of the student's thinking.</q>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The framework motivates the analytic categories, but the project analyzes
        evidence in written responses rather than claiming to model students'
        complete conceptions.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 2: CODER'S HANDBOOK                                  -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-coders-handbook">
    <title>Coder's Handbook</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <term>Version 0.1</term> — <today />. This handbook will be revised as we
        code. When a rule changes, the version number changes, and your coding 
        sheets record which version you coded under.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-student-guide-episode">
      <title>The Episode Model</title>

      <p>
        A student response may contain one or more justifications. Moving from one 
        justification to the next is called an <term>episode</term>. For some
        response <m>x</m>, the ordered sequence of episodes is given by
        <md>
          E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_n\rangle.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Each episode has the form
        <md>
          e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k),
        </md>
        where:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>C_k</m> is the <term>criterion</term>: a fact, observation,
            condition, or feature of the situation the student <em>cites</em>
            (what they point at);
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>I_k</m> is the <term>inferential link</term>: The (usually unstated) 
            inference connecting the criterion to the verdict; and
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>J_k</m> is the <term>judgment</term>: The verdict the student <em>draws</em>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        In brief:
        <md>
          \text{criterion}
          \quad \xrightarrow{\text{inferential link}} \quad
          \text{judgment}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the response:
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.</p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            The criterion, inferential link, and judgment could be identified as:
            <dl width="narrow">
              <li>
                <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>
                <p><q>There is a free variable</q> (A free variable exists)</p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>
                <p>A system with a free variable cannot have one unique solution (free variable exists <m>\Rightarrow</m> solution not unique)</p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>
                <p><q>not possible to have a unique solution</q> (solution is not unique).</p>
              </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        An episode records only the justification visible in the response. It does
        not describe everything the student knew, did, or thought.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="rem-handbook-crux">
        <p>
          An episode is a <term>visible trace of justification in the writing</term>.
          It is <em>not</em> a claim about what the student understands, believes,
          or <q>has in their head.</q> We code what is on the page. Nothing more.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-episode-project-model">
      <title>Why Use Episodes?</title>

      <p>
        The Episode Model is motivated by the cK&#162; framework, especially its
        treatment of control structures and validation. However,
        <m>(C,I,J)</m> is not part of Balacheff's original model.
      </p>

      <p>
        A conception is represented by the broader quadruplet
        <m>(P,R,L,\Sigma)</m>. An episode records only a criterion, a link, and a
        judgment. It therefore provides evidence related to a control structure,
        not a complete conception.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-episode-evidence">
      <title>Finding the Three Parts</title>

      <p>
        The criterion, link, and judgment may appear in one sentence. Words such
        as <q>because</q>, <q>since</q>, <q>so</q>, and <q>therefore</q> often
        signal the connection.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The system is inconsistent, so the vector is not in the span.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Here:
        <ul>
          <li>criterion: the system is inconsistent;</li>
          <li>
            link: an inconsistent system has no solution, so the vector cannot be
            written as a linear combination of the given vectors; and
          </li>
          <li>judgment: the vector is not in the span.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Connector words are clues, not guarantees. A clear inference may appear
        without one, and a response may use <q>because</q> without giving a valid
        connection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Coders use three evidence levels:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Explicit</title>
            <p>The component is directly stated.</p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Strongly supported</title>
            <p>
              The component is unstated but clear from the response without
              adding new reasoning.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Missing or ambiguous</title>
            <p>Identifying the component would require guessing or repair.</p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        On the coding sheet, these correspond to <c>explicit</c>,
        <c>implicit</c>, and <c>absent</c>. An absent link makes the episode
        incomplete.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-complete-episode">
      <title>Complete and Incomplete Episodes</title>

      <p>
        A <term>complete episode</term> contains enough evidence to identify the
        criterion, link, and judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        An <term>incomplete episode</term> lacks one or more parts. For example:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There is a free variable.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This gives a criterion but no judgment. The student might mean that the
        solution is not unique, that there are infinitely many solutions, or
        something else. The coder must not choose a conclusion for the student.
      </p>

      <p>
        Missing information remains missing. Incomplete episodes may be preserved
        as partial evidence, but they do not receive the correctness code used for
        complete episodes.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-multiple-episodes">
      <title>Responses with More Than One Episode</title>

      <p>
        A response may contain several episodes. Consider:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are fewer pivots than variables, so there is a free variable.
          Therefore, the solution cannot be unique.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This response contains two chained episodes:
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
            e_1 \amp: \text{fewer pivots than variables}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{a free variable exists},\\
            e_2 \amp: \text{a free variable exists}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{the solution is not unique}.
          \end{aligned}
        </md>
        The judgment of <m>e_1</m> becomes the criterion of <m>e_2</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Episodes may be:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Chained</title>
            <p>The judgment of one becomes the criterion of another.</p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Independent</title>
            <p>
              Separate justifications support the same conclusion, such as a
              pivot argument and a dimension argument.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Nested</title>
            <p>One episode serves as a sub-step inside a larger argument.</p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Nested episodes require a consistent project policy: code both levels,
        only the larger episode, or only the most specific episode. This is a
        coding convention, not a mathematical requirement.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-local-global">
      <title>Local Reasoning and the Complete Answer</title>

      <p>
        A valid episode does not guarantee a complete answer. Suppose a question
        asks whether a solution is unique and asks the student to identify the
        basic and free variables. The response
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are two pivots, so one variable is free.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        contains a sound local episode:
        <md>
          \text{two pivots among three variables}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{one variable is free}.
        </md>
        However, it does not identify the free variable or state whether the
        solution is unique.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project therefore evaluates:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <term>justification quality</term>: whether the episode is
            mathematically sound; and
          </li>
          <li>
            <term>goal coverage</term>: whether the response completes every task.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A response may contain sound but incomplete reasoning, or reach the
        requested answer through an invalid inference.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-operations">
      <title>What does not get coded</title>

      <p>
        Student writing contains two kinds of move:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Operation</title>
            <p>
              It builds or transforms an object. Row-reducing a
              matrix, solving for <m>x</m>, computing a count (<q>I counted 2
              pivots</q>). Operations are <em>not</em> coded as episodes.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Control</title>
            <p>
              It reads a feature of the current state and returns
              a verdict (<q>there's a free variable, so it's not unique</q>). Controls
              <em>are</em> what episodes record.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-finding">
      <title>Finding episodes in real writing</title>

      <p>
        Practical signals:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Connector words</title>
            <p>
              Words like <em>so</em>, <em>because</em>, <em>since</em>, <em>therefore</em>, 
              etc. signal an inference link. Look for these connector words, but do not assume 
              that a response is incomplete without them.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Multiple criteria</title>
            <p>
              If a student cites two different features and draws a verdict from each, 
              that's two episodes.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Bare assertions</title>
            <p>
              The response <q>there is a free variable</q> with no verdict drawn from it,
              are not complete episodes. Record them as incomplete.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-two-digit">
      <title>The two-digit correctness code</title>

      <p>
        Complete episodes receive the two-part correctness code (adapted from Gerami
        et al. 2024)
        <md>
          \operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2).
        </md>
        where the digits, <m>c_k^1</m> and <m>c_k^2</m>, are determined as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Is the criterion true?</title>
            <p>
              We score <m>c_k^1 = 1</m> if the criterion they cited is true, and 
              <m>c_k^1 = 0</m> otherwise. It is judged against the actual mathematical
              state of the problem. If the student says <q>there is a free variable</q>
              and, in fact, there is, then <m>c_k^1 = 1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Is the inference valid?</title>
            <p>
              We score <m>c_k^2 = 1</m> if the inference is valid, and <m>c_k^2 = 0</m> 
              otherwise.
            </p>
            <blockquote>
              <p>
                Free variable (in a consistent system) <m>\Rightarrow</m> not a unique solution
              </p>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              is mathematically valid, so <m>c_k^2 = 1</m>. Whereas
            </p>
            <blockquote>
              <p>
                # variables <m>&gt;</m> # equations <m>\Rightarrow</m> <m>\infty</m>-many solutions
              </p>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              is <em>not</em> valid in general so <m>c_k^2 = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Remember:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Incomplete episodes are never scored.</term> No judgment, or no
              criterion, means leave the scores blank (not <m>(0,0)</m>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>A <m>(1,1)</m> score certifies the justification, not the answer.</term> A
              response can be all-<m>(1,1)</m> episodes and still fail to answer the
              question. We are grading only the reasoning moves.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-nesting">
      <title>Chained and Independent Episodes</title>

      <p>The student response</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>One and only one solution is not possible because there will be a free variable since there are more variables than rows.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        contains two <term>chained</term> episodes since the criterion of <m>e_2</m>
        <em>is</em> the judgment of <m>e_1</m>. That is:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="center"/>
        <col />
        <col />
        <row>
          <cell><m>E</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><m>C</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><m>J</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>e_1</m></cell>
          <cell><q>more variables than rows</q></cell>
          <cell><q>there will be a free variable</q></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>e_2</m></cell>
          <cell><q>there will be a free variable</q></cell>
          <cell><q>one and only one solution is not possible</q></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        In contrast, when a response gives two <em>separate</em>, non-interacting
        justifications, we say that the episodes are <term>independent</term>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-confidence">
      <title>Scoring confidence</title>

      <p>
        The pipeline records a confidence value with every automated decision, and
        so do you (the <c>confidence</c> column). Low confidence plus a clear note 
        is good coding.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, what confidence is <em>about</em>. It records your confidence in the
        <em>coding decision</em>. That is, how confident you are in extracting an 
        episode and how to fill a single row, <em>not</em> whether the student is 
        mathematically right.
      </p>

      <p>
        Confidence is logged on a three-point scale:
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>3: unambiguous</title>
            <p>
              Any coder would code it the same way; there is no plausible alternative
              reading; the completeness and scoring are clear.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>2: an alternative may exist</title>
            <p>
              You have a clear reading, but a coder could read it differently: 
              an ambiguous connector, one-episode-vs-two, a borderline 
              complete/incomplete call, or a <m>c^1</m>/<m>c^2</m> judgment that
              hinges on a subtle point.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>1: genuinely unsure</title>
            <p>
              You are torn between readings, or you doubt the framework has a clean
              slot for this response at all.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Three practices make the number mean something:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Say what is uncertain.</term> Add a note that names your doubt:
              segmentation, completeness, or the validity of the link.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Be consistent.</term> Coder consistency matters more than matching
              the other coder's number. You are each calibrating your own scale.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Treat it as triage.</term> Every 1 and 2 opens up discussion.
              Confidence is a flag, not a measurement.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-sheet">
      <title>What goes in your coding sheet</title>

      <p>
        Column spec for episode-level coding (template:
        <c>templates/episode-coding-sheet.csv</c>). One <term>row per episode</term>,
        so a response with two episodes gets two rows.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-handbook-columns">
        <title>Columns of the episode coding sheet.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="22%"/>
          <col width="78%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Column</cell>
            <cell>What to enter</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>response_id</c></cell>
            <cell>The ID supplied with the data (copy exactly).</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>episode_n</c></cell>
            <cell>1, 2, 3, … within this response, in the order the <em>judgments</em> appear.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>criterion_verbatim</c></cell>
            <cell>The student's words for <m>C</m>, copy-pasted.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>judgment_verbatim</c></cell>
            <cell>The student's words for <m>J</m>, copy-pasted.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>link_stated</c></cell>
            <cell><c>explicit</c> (a connector word is present), <c>implicit</c> (the move is clearly made but no connector), or <c>absent</c> (no verdict follows the criterion, so the episode is <em>incomplete</em>).</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>complete</c></cell>
            <cell>1 if the episode has both a criterion and a judgment; 0 otherwise.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>c1</c></cell>
            <cell>Correctness digit 1 (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-two-digit" text="title"/>). Leave blank for incomplete episodes.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>c2</c></cell>
            <cell>Correctness digit 2 (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-two-digit" text="title"/>). Leave blank for incomplete episodes.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>relation</c></cell>
            <cell>Blank for a lone episode; <c>chained(k)</c> if this episode's criterion is episode <m>k</m>'s judgment; <c>independent</c> if it stands apart from the others (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-nesting" text="title"/>).</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>code</c></cell>
            <cell>The control-structure code</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>oov_note</c></cell>
            <cell>Any terminology that feels outside the course's standard way of talking about this topic. Copy the term verbatim (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-edge-cases" text="title"/>).</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>confidence</c></cell>
            <cell>3 = confident; 2 = plausible but arguable; 1 = genuinely unsure.</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><c>notes</c></cell>
            <cell>Anything a future reader of this row needs. Never leave a <c>confidence</c>=1 row without a note.</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-handbook-edge-cases">
      <title>Edge-case Rules of Thumb</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-handbook-edge-cases">
        <title>What to do with common non-standard responses.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="34%"/>
          <col width="66%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>You see…</cell>
            <cell>Do this</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Blank / whitespace only</term></cell>
            <cell>One row: <c>complete</c>=0, note <q>blank.</q> No episode content.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Bare answer, no justification</term> (<q>No.</q> / <q>Not possible.</q>)</cell>
            <cell>One row: <c>judgment_verbatim</c> filled, no criterion, <c>complete</c>=0, note <q>bare answer.</q></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Restates the question</term> or <q>I don't know</q></cell>
            <cell>One row, <c>complete</c>=0, note what happened.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Pure computation, no verdict</term> (<q>I row reduced and got x=2, y=3</q>)</cell>
            <cell>Operations only, but watch for a hidden episode: <q>…so yes, there is one solution</q> turns the computation's <em>result</em> into a criterion. If genuinely no verdict: <c>complete</c>=0, note <q>operations only.</q></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Hedged / conditional reasoning</term> (<q>It depends what is in the augmented matrix</q>)</cell>
            <cell>This <em>is</em> often a codable move (the student's criterion is that the given information underdetermines the answer). Code it as an episode if a criterion and judgment are identifiable; confidence 1–2 and a note are expected here.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Unfamiliar or off-standard terminology</term> (e.g., invoking determinants, kernels, or other machinery outside how the course discusses this topic)</cell>
            <cell>Code the episode structure normally; copy the term into <c>oov_note</c> verbatim. Do not translate the student's term into more standard language.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Citation of a theorem by number</term> (<q>by Proposition 1.4.4</q>)</cell>
            <cell>The citation is the criterion. Record it verbatim; put the proposition reference in <c>oov_note</c>; do not chase down the proposition's content unless instructed.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Correct answer, invalid link</term></cell>
            <cell>Ordinary case: code it, expect <m>c^1</m> and/or <m>c^2 = 0</m> per <xref ref="subsec-handbook-two-digit" text="title"/>. Resist the halo effect of a right answer.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><term>Two readings both feel defensible</term></cell>
            <cell>Code your primary reading; describe the alternative in <c>notes</c>; confidence at most 2.</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><term>You want to ask the other coder</term></cell>
            <cell>Don't (during independent passes). Ask [PI NAME] if it's a rules question; otherwise note it and move on.</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 3: EXERCISE: EPISODE IDENTIFICATION                  -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-exercise-episode-warmup">
    <title>Episode Identification Practice</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The goal is to practice the one skill everything else builds on: read
        a response, break it into <term>episodes</term>: criterion (<m>C</m>),
        inferential link (<m>I</m>), judgment (<m>J</m>), defined in the
        <xref ref="sec-coders-handbook" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-setup">
      <title>The Setup</title>

      <p>
        All five responses answer the same question (actual system omitted):
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <q>Can this system have exactly one solution? Why or why not?</q>
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        For <em>this</em> question, the correct analysis is:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The system is <term>consistent</term> and has <term>more variables
          than equations</term>, so it has a <term>free variable</term>, and
          therefore it has <term>infinitely many solutions</term>. So it
          <em>cannot</em> have exactly one solution.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Treat that as the true state of the mathematics when you judge the
        two correctness digits using
        <xref ref="subsec-handbook-two-digit" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-responses">
      <title>The five responses (made up for this exercise)</title>

      <p>
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>STU-A</title>
            <p><q>
              There is a free variable, so it cannot have exactly one solution.
            </q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-B</title>
            <p><q>
              There are more variables than equations, so there will be a 
              free variable, so it can't have exactly one solution.
            </q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-C</title>
            <p><q>
              There are more variables than equations, so there are infinitely
              many solutions.
            </q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-D</title>
            <p><q>There is a free variable in this system.</q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-E</title>
            <p><q>No.</q></p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-produce">
      <title>What to produce</title>

      <p>
        Use <c>templates/episode-coding-sheet.csv</c> from the shared folder.
        <term>One row per episode</term>, so a response with two episodes gets
        two rows, and a response with none still gets one row recording that.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fill out all columns except: <c>code</c>, <c>oov_note</c>, 
        <c>handbook_version</c>, and <c>codebook_version</c>. For how to score
        the <c>confidence</c> column, see
        <xref ref="subsec-handbook-confidence" text="title"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        For clarity, here is a worked row for <term>STU-A</term>:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-warmup-example">
        <title>Worked coding row for STU-A.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="24%"/>
          <col width="76%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Column</cell>
            <cell>Value for STU-A</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>response_id</c></cell>
            <cell>STU-A</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>episode_n</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>criterion_verbatim</c></cell>
            <cell>there is a free variable</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>judgment_verbatim</c></cell>
            <cell>it cannot have exactly one solution</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>link_stated</c></cell>
            <cell><c>explicit</c></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>complete</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>c1</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>c2</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>relation</c></cell>
            <cell>(blank)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>confidence</c></cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><c>notes</c></cell>
            <cell>
              free var is true of this system; free var <m>\Rightarrow</m> not
              exactly one solution is a valid step regardless of consistency
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <!-- <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-watch">
      <title>Things to watch for</title>

      <p>
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>STU-B</title>
            <p>
              More than one episode. Where does one end and the next begin,
              and how do you record that the second one's criterion is the
              first one's judgment?
              (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-nesting" text="title"/>.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-C</title>
            <p>
              The tricky one. Its conclusion is the <em>right answer for this
              system</em>. Ask yourself whether the criterion <em>by 
              itself</em> forces that conclusion, or whether a system with
              <q>more variables than equations</q> could have landed somewhere
              else. Let that decide <m>c^2</m>. Confidence 2 and a note is a
