            <q>there are infinitely many solutions</q>. <m>c^1 = 1</m> (true of this
            system). <m>c^2 = 0</m>: <q>more variables than equations</q> does not
            entail <q>infinitely many solutions</q> (the system could be
            inconsistent, so the criterion does not force that conclusion). The
            conclusion is correct for this particular system, but <m>c^2</m> grades
            the inference, not the answer (resist the halo effect
            of a right answer). This is the response most likely to divide coders on
            how strict to be about the missing consistency premise, so confidence 2
            with a note is the right way to record it, and it goes to adjudication.
            Contrast STU-A: the same family of criterion, but STU-A's weaker
            conclusion (<q>not exactly one</q>) is robust to consistency while
            STU-C's stronger conclusion (<q>infinitely many</q>) is not.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
